To implement doGet and doPost methods for a hotel management project in Java, 
you typically use a servlet to handle HTTP requests and responses. Below is a 
simple example of how to implement these methods in a servlet, assuming you are building
a hotel management system where you want to display hotel room details on a GET 
request and allow users to make bookings (through POST).

Example Servlet: HotelManagementServlet.java
import javax.servlet.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class HotelManagementServlet extends HttpServlet {

    // For simplicity, we store hotel data (rooms and bookings) in memory.
    // In a real application, this should be stored in a database.
    private Map<Integer, String> rooms = new HashMap<>();
    private List<String> bookings = new ArrayList<>();

    @Override
    public void init() throws ServletException {
        // Initialize some hotel room data.
        rooms.put(101, "Single Room - $100 per night");
        rooms.put(102, "Double Room - $150 per night");
        rooms.put(103, "Suite - $250 per night");
    }

    // Handle GET requests (e.g., displaying available rooms)
    @Override
    protected void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws ServletException, IOException {
        response.setContentType("text/html");

        // Print available rooms
        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
        out.println("<html><body>");
        out.println("<h1>Hotel Room List</h1>");
        out.println("<table border='1'><tr><th>Room Number</th><th>Room Details</th></tr>");

        for (Map.Entry<Integer, String> room : rooms.entrySet()) {
            out.println("<tr><td>" + room.getKey() + "</td><td>" + room.getValue() + "</td></tr>");
        }
        
        out.println("</table>");
        out.println("<h2>Make a Booking</h2>");
        out.println("<form method='POST'>");
        out.println("<label for='roomNumber'>Enter Room Number to Book:</label>");
        out.println("<input type='text' id='roomNumber' name='roomNumber' required>");
        out.println("<button type='submit'>Book Room</button>");
        out.println("</form>");
        out.println("</body></html>");
    }

    // Handle POST requests (e.g., booking a room)
    @Override
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String roomNumberStr = request.getParameter("roomNumber");
        PrintWriter out = response.getWriter();
        response.setContentType("text/html");

        if (roomNumberStr != null && !roomNumberStr.isEmpty()) {
            try {
                int roomNumber = Integer.parseInt(roomNumberStr);
                if (rooms.containsKey(roomNumber)) {
                    // Room booking successful
                    bookings.add("Room " + roomNumber + " booked.");
                    out.println("<html><body>");
                    out.println("<h1>Booking Confirmation</h1>");
                    out.println("<p>Room " + roomNumber + " has been successfully booked!</p>");
                    out.println("<a href=''>Back to room list</a>");
                    out.println("</body></html>");
                } else {
                    // Room number invalid
                    out.println("<html><body>");
                    out.println("<h1>Error</h1>");
                    out.println("<p>Sorry, room number " + roomNumber + " is not available.</p>");
                    out.println("<a href=''>Back to room list</a>");
                    out.println("</body></html>");
                }
            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                // Invalid room number format
                out.println("<html><body>");
                out.println("<h1>Error</h1>");
                out.println("<p>Invalid room number format. Please enter a valid number.</p>");
                out.println("<a href=''>Back to room list</a>");
                out.println("</body></html>");
            }
        } else {
            out.println("<html><body>");
            out.println("<h1>Error</h1>");
            out.println("<p>Please provide a room number to book.</p>");
            out.println("<a href=''>Back to room list</a>");
            out.println("</body></html>");
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void destroy() {
        // Cleanup resources, if needed.
    }
}

Key Points:
doGet Method:

1-This method is responsible for handling GET requests. It displays a list of available rooms.
2-It retrieves the available rooms (in this case, stored in the rooms map) and presents them in an HTML table format.
3-A simple form is included where the user can enter the room number to make a booking.

doPost Method:

1-This method handles POST requests. When the user submits the booking form (with a room number), the doPost method is triggered.
2-It checks whether the room number entered by the user exists in the rooms map.
3-If the room number exists, it confirms the booking; otherwise, it displays an error message.


