<web-app xmlns="http://java.sun.com/xml/ns/javaee" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://java.sun.com/xml/ns/javaee http://java.sun.com/xml/ns/javaee/web-app_3_1.xsd" version="3.1">
    <servlet>
        <servlet-name>HotelManagementServlet</servlet-name>
        <servlet-class>HotelManagementServlet</servlet-class>
    </servlet>
    <servlet-mapping>
        <servlet-name>HotelManagementServlet</servlet-name>
        <url-pattern>/hotel</url-pattern>
    </servlet-mapping>
</web-app>


Servlet Mapping (Optional, for annotations-based configuration):
If you're using servlet annotations (since Servlet 3.0), you can replace the web.xml configuration with:

@WebServlet("/hotel")
public class HotelManagementServlet extends HttpServlet {
    // your servlet code here
}

Conclusion:
In this simple example, the doGet method retrieves and displays hotel room data to the user, and
the doPost method handles the booking logic by checking whether the room exists and confirming the booking. 
You can extend this functionality 
with more sophisticated features like user authentication, room availability checks, and database integration.
